comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-16T11:30:44.053Z","harness:claude-code","Worked escape, 2026-08-16, useful as a concrete case for this ratchet's design.\n\nEscape: commit 4454ca930 landed on main with 99.99 percent line, statement and branch coverage and blocked Gates (coverage). Three branches introduced by that commit had no test reaching them: the early return in validateSkillReferenceReachability when a skill ships no references directory, the null-target filter when a SKILL.md link does not resolve to a path, and the non-object export entry in listPublishedCodeExports. Fixed in 816ceb0d3; local exact coverage back to 100/100/100/100 over 506 files and 7,410 tests.\n\nWhat made it escape is the point for this item. The change carried targeted specs and both of them passed - 17 for the surface snapshot and 22 for the docs gate - and node scripts/run-tests.mjs test passed in full. The coverage gate is the only surface that would have caught it, and it is the one local step a scoped change has no incentive to run because it costs about six minutes where the targeted spec costs seconds.\n\nSo the gate that would have caught this already exists; what is missing is that nothing tells a contributor their diff introduced an uncovered branch until CI says so. The cheap form of this ratchet is therefore not a new gate but a diff-scoped coverage check: run coverage over the changed files only and report uncovered lines within the diff, which is seconds rather than minutes and would have named all three branches before the push.\n\nSecond-order note for pm-2qqcgl, which owns the fact that run-tests.mjs coverage prints the shortfall and exits 0: in this run the wrapper printed both 'Test execution failed; exact coverage still passed.' and 'Tests passed; exact coverage failed.' followed by 'EXIT:0' in the same output, from different sub-steps, while the final combined verdict was correct. An agent reading that log with a grep can extract either verdict. The final line is the only trustworthy one."
  "2026-08-16T19:27:27.594Z","harness:codex","Completion evidence 2026-08-16: defect-evidence gate PASS; all four escape classes are represented in historical closed items with structured gate_added/gate_strengthened evidence, while the post-epoch governed set is empty. The negative control creates a post-epoch closed Issue without escape_class or gate_evidence and exits 1 with both actionable findings. Linked tests passed 10/10; repository assurance provider passed with its negative control proven."
  "2026-08-16T20:01:01.202Z","harness:codex","Delivery PR: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/pull/1043. The PR implements the escape-class schema, evidence policy, cross-source historical classification, negative controls, and recurrence budgets tracked by this task."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-31T19:42:23.546Z","harness:claude-code","First worked example recorded 2026-07-31, offered as a concrete calibration case rather than as a change of scope.\n\nThis task states the ratchet as policy: every escaped defect must land a gate that would have caught it. What it has lacked is an instance where the policy is checked against history and found to have been violated, which is what makes the ratchet worth building rather than merely agreeing with.\n\nThe instance is pm-qtbc, closed with resolution \"Fixed pm-mcp main-module detection by realpath-resolving symlinked npm bin argv paths.\" It is an ideal test case for three reasons.\n\nIt genuinely escaped. The defect reached a published artifact and was found by dogfooding, per its dogfood-2026-06-12 tag, not by any gate.\n\nIt landed no gate. grep -rn \"pm-mcp\" over scripts/release/ and .github/workflows/ returns zero matches at HEAD b7651d793, so the entrypoint that shipped dead is still executed by nothing in the pipeline. A regression would ship green today. That absence is now filed as pm-u0oz2k.\n\nIt is the hard case for gate design, not the easy one. The failure was a silent exit 0 with no output, reachable only under a symlinked argv[1] - so it is invisible from an in-repo run and invisible to an exit-code or --version probe. A ratchet that only asks \"was a test added\" would have accepted a unit test that could never have caught this. The requirement the ratchet needs to express is stronger: the gate must fail on a reconstruction of the original defect, which is a negative control rather than a coverage delta.\n\nTwo suggestions for this task's acceptance criteria follow from that, both cheap.\n\nFirst, the ratchet should demand a negative control, not a gate. \"A gate exists\" is satisfiable by a gate that cannot fail - which is exactly the live condition pm-lpqln4 documents for the bunx probe, where an arbitrary token passes. Confirmed again today: node dist/cli.js ZZZ_NOT_A_COMMAND --version prints the version and exits 0.\n\nSecond, the ratchet should apply retroactively to already-closed defects as a bounded backfill, because the population of escaped-and-ungated defects is finite and enumerable from resolution text plus closed_at. Applying it only to future escapes leaves the existing holes permanently unmeasured, and pm-qtbc shows those holes sit on the agent-facing surface rather than in peripheral code."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-16T19:27:28.249Z","harness:codex","Separate historical classification from epoch-governed enforcement: old incidents provide recurrence evidence, while only defects completed after the declared epoch fail closed on missing structured gate proof."
